<article class="grid max-w-5xl gap-6">
    {{ template "hero.gohtml" . }}

<p>go-importmap fetches JavaScript and CSS package files from CDNs and generates import-map and stylesheet markup. Packages can be vendored at build time and embedded in the binary, so the application needs no network at run time. It serves their files with versioned URLs, strong ETags and Subresource Integrity, and your application chooses which JavaScript modules to import.</p>
<h2>Two ways to use it</h2>
<ul><li>Vendor at build time, embed at run time (recommended). The <code>importmap</code> command downloads the packages into a directory with their licences and a lock file. The program embeds that directory and loads it with <code>FromFS</code>.</li><li>Fetch at start-up. <code>CacheOrFetch</code> downloads missing packages into <code>.importmap/</code>, publishes them in <code>assets/</code> and maps them. Handy while developing.</li></ul>
<h2>The asset workflow</h2>
<ol><li>Declare packages, versions and required files.</li><li>Vendor them at build time, or fetch missing files at start-up.</li><li>Include the generated head before your module scripts.</li><li>Serve the files from your application.</li></ol>
<pre><code>import htmx from "htmx";
window.htmx = htmx;</code></pre>
<p>An import map gives <code>htmx</code> a URL; importing the module executes it. The map alone does not initialize a library.</p>
